.TH BUFFER_WRITEFILE 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
buffer_writefile \- Write the contents of a buffer to a file

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/buffer.h>

.IB int
.IB buffer_writefile (buffer* b, const char* fn);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBbuffer_writefile\fR function is used to write the contents of a buffer \fBb\fR to a file specified by the file name \fBfn\fR. It opens the file in write mode and writes the buffer contents to the file.

The function returns 0 on success, or -1 if an error occurred.

The \fBbuffer_writefile\fR function is intended to be used to save the contents of a buffer to a file, such as when writing data to a log file or a configuration file.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBbuffer_writefile\fR function to write the contents of a buffer to a file:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/buffer.h>

int main() {
    buffer* b = buffer_new();
    buffer_append(b, "Hello, world!\n");

    const char* filename = "example.txt";
    int result = buffer_writefile(b, filename);
    if(result == -1) {
        perror("buffer_writefile");
        buffer_free(b);
        return 1;
    }

    buffer_free(b);
    return 0;
}
.nef